  "name": "Kapela podwórkowa (Warsaw courtyard street-song band)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Street and courtyard ballad of working-class Warsaw, from districts such as Czerniaków and Praga, sung for coins in tenement courtyards and at taverns by small bands of accordion, banjo or mandolin and violin in the city's own street dialect. Its humorous, sentimental and underworld songs passed into recording around 1960 through Stanisław Grzesiuk, who accompanied himself on banjo and mandolin for Polish Radio and Polskie Nagrania, and later through bands such as Kapela Czerniakowska.",
  "recipe": "classic early-1960s kapela podwórkowa, ballad Poetry, Slavic bard branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment. accordion, credited banjo unspecified, traditional mellow bowl back Italian mandolin, fiddle. urban courtyard. small-diaphragm pencil condenser, portable field-recorder pre. tape 15ips.",
